jpmorgan chase is by far the largest  traditional bank in the us and one of  the most well-known and respected banks  in the world  many people despise chase because they  are quote-unquote too big to fail  meaning that they can get away with  nearly any financial mistake but how big  exactly is jpmorgan chase  well at the end of 2020 jpmorgan chase  reported their total deposits and client  assets to be a whopping  5.926 trillion dollars  but just a decade ago the bank's assets  and deposits stood at less than half the  current amount at 2.8 trillion  and whether you respect the company or  not this is an extremely impressive  figure and the man behind this  phenomenal growth is none other than  jamie dimon so here's the story of how  jamie dimon became the 5.9 trillion  dollar banker  taking a look back jamie dimon was born  on march 13 1956 in new york city to  greek immigrants  if you're familiar with european last  names you might have noticed that  jaime's last name diamond is not a greek  name but rather a french name the  family's original last name was actually  papa dimitrio but jaime's grandfather  decided to change the name to diamond in  order to sound more french his  grandfather was also a banker who worked  in athens and i guess he thought that  sounding french would make him more  trustworthy i'm not quite sure but  anyway going back to jaime it looks like  he had a pretty nice childhood as his  father was an executive vice president  at american express jaime ended up  attending the browning school followed  by tufts university where he majored in  psychology and economics during his time  at tufts jaime wrote an essay about the  creation of cheerson through a plethora  of mergers at the time shirsen was the  second largest security firm on wall  street and jaime's mother would mail  jaime's essay to sandy wale who was the  leader of shearson sandy offered jaime a  summer internship at the firm which  would eventually turn into a two-year  stint as a management consultant jaime  would end up returning to school though  to pursue an mba from harvard business  school it looks like jaime was a star  student at harvard given that he would  end up graduating as a baker scholar  which means that he was in the top five  percent of his class during both years  of his mba in between his two years at  harvard jaime also picked up a summer  internship at goldman sachs considering  his education and all of these highly  respected internships i don't think  you'd be surprised to hear that he had  several job offers waiting for him when  he graduated in 1982. these offers  included goldman sachs morgan stanley  and lehman brothers  but jaime ended up choosing american  express even though they offered the  lowest salary of the bunch sandy whale  had moved over from shearson to american  express and jaime had the opportunity to  work with sandy again at american  express which jaime valued more than the  higher salaries at the other firms  between 1982 and 1995 jaime worked as  sandy's assistant and when sandy left in  1985 jaime would follow him to his next  venture sandy had his eye on a failing  consumer finance company called  commercial credit sandy acquired the  company and took a private and appointed  jaime as the cfo  over the next few years jaime and sandy  bought a couple of smaller financial  institutions that were in a much better  state  this included primerica financial  services the life insurance company a.l  williams and the brokerage firm smith  barney  following the acquisitions the duo  implemented a cross-selling strategy  which meant that each entity within the  parent company would buy services from  another entity under the parent company  whenever possible  this minimized the total outflow from  the overall company and vastly improved  the financial state of the parent  company  by the early 1990s commercial credit was  doing so well that jaime took a stab at  acquiring a failing institution called  travelers insurance  in september of 1992 travelers insurance  was on the brink of bankruptcy because  hurricane andrew had just devastated the  bahamas florida and louisiana which  resulted in a massive influx of  insurance claims  it was a rather risky bet though as  travelers insurance was not a small  company coming in at 5 billion  but if they could stay solvent after the  acquisition the new company would be one  of the largest financial services firms  in the u.s  jaime and sandy would not only take the  risk but they would also take the name  travelers as the brand was far more  recognized both in commercial credit and  primarica around the same time the duo  would acquire shearson for 1 billion  dollars which is quite ironic given that  jamie was just an entry-level intern at  the company about 15 years before this  all of this was just the beginning  though as sandy and jamie were about to  create one of the largest banks in the  world and i'm not talking about chase in  1997 travelers would acquire the famous  investment bank solomon brothers for  nine billion dollars  this was the second largest acquisition  in banking history at the time but the  merger that would take place the  following year would blow this deal out  of the water  in 1998 travelers group made a deal with  citicorp to merge the two companies  both companies were valued equally at 70  billion dollars meaning that the new  firm would stand at 140 billion dollars  and that's how modern day city group was  founded the chairman of either company  sandy whale and john reed became co-ceos  of the new company and jaime was  appointed as the president of citigroup  jaime was only 42 years old at this  point and he was already the president  of one of the largest banks in the world  but unfortunately things weren't as good  as they seemed on the surface sandy and  jaime had gotten into a bit of a  misunderstanding the story goes that  sandy was salty that jaime didn't  promote sandy's children up the ranks of  the company sandy expected jaime to  point his daughter jessica as the chief  of asset management and his son mark as  the leader of the solomon bond business  sandy was about to retire in the next  couple of years and the expectation was  that jaime would take over as ceo but  sandy feared that if jaime became ceo  his children would be left in the dust  so sandy felt that he had to make a  choice between his adopted son jaime and  his real children jessica and mark  honestly i feel like this was a pretty  unfair expectation i don't think jaime  was against promoting jessica and mark  by any means he just wanted them to rise  up due to merit as opposed to family  connections sandy obviously disagreed  with this dance and at an executive  retreat sandy would ask jaime to resign  from the company when the news broke  that jaime was resigning wall street was  shocked  apparently when jaime stepped onto the  solomon smith barney training floor  after resigning the 1000 traitors in the  room gave him a standing ovation clearly  jaime was a highly respected man in the  industry and there was no objective  reason to fire him  it should also be noted that jessica and  mark would end up leaving citigroup so  sandy's decision was utterly pointless  jaime was unfortunately not able to  become the ceo of cd group but we all  know that it would end up playing out  even better for jaime jamie didn't  instantly get another job though he  would take a 18-month break from work  and he would actually consider retiring  jaime says that he was very open-minded  about the situation he had plenty of  money so money wasn't a concern  all of his children were quite young so  he considered staying at home and  raising them jaime also received plenty  of job offers from leading companies  jeff bezos actually offered jaime the  position of amazon president  if jaime had taken the job he might have  been amazon ceo today but jamie decided  against it deciding that finance was his  bread and butter when the opportunity to  save a feeling bank came up though jaime  knew that this was his calling jaime  took on a job at bank one as ceo and  chairman in march of 2000  at the time bank 1 was the 5th largest  bank in the us but they were getting  crushed during the dotcom bubble the  bank started losing money in 1999 and in  2000 they would end up losing 511  million dollars so clearly the situation  was not good the first thing jaime did  was buy 2 million shares of the company  because he thought that he would be more  motivated to save the company when he  had money invested after that jaime went  on a waste reduction purge he cut  relationships with bad partners shut  down the unprofitable online division  and he made branch manager compensation  based on net revenue as opposed to total  sales  jamie was able to save one billion  dollars and make bank one a respected  financial institution once again and  soon enough jaime would land on the deal  of his lifetime in january of 2004 jaime  would negotiate a deal with jp morgan  chase to complete a merger of equals  citigroup had become the world's largest  financial services firm and the j.p  morgan chase needed the merger to  compete against citigroup so they would  agree and the two companies would merge  in june of 2004. jaime was originally  appointed as the president and ceo of  jpmorgan chase however once the ceo  william harrison retired in 2006 jaime  would move up to being ceo and one year  later he would become chairman as well  unfortunately the 2008 financial crisis  would roll around the following year and  jaime was at the home when chase was  quote-unquote bailed out the media often  likes to paint chase as needing the 25  billion bailout that they received but  this is really not the case jp morgan  chase was the strongest of all the  american banks in fact they didn't even  have a single negative quarter during  the entire crisis  jaime says that the only reason they  took the bailout is because the u.s  treasury wanted them to the treasury  felt that if chase took the bailout  smaller banks who actually needed the  bailout would feel more comfortable in  taking it as well  also chase paid back the entire amount  just one year later in june of 2009  around the same time jp morgan chase  also became the largest american bank  overtaking cd group and with that jaime  had become the largest american banker  this time jaime didn't get screwed over  by chase  in fact he would be extremely well  compensated and even become a  billionaire in 2015  but unfortunately he would be hit with a  new set of problems in 2014 jaime was  diagnosed with throat cancer and he had  to go through radiation and chemotherapy  for several weeks and just last year  jaime nearly died after his aorta  exploded the most ironic part of this  terrible experience was that when jaime  caught a taxi to go to the nearest  hospital after feeling severe chest pain  the taxi driver drove him to will  cornell medical center which was of  course named after sandy whale  fortunately the doctors were able to  repair his aorta with the seven hour  surgery and jaime was able to return to  work the following month today jamie has  until 2023 as ceo at which point his  term expires  he'll likely have the opportunity to  extend his term for another 5 years  but he's already chosen some successors  so jaime's time at chase may be coming  to an end very soon  do you guys think it's about time that  jaime got out or do you think that jaime  was a phenomenal ceo comment that down  below  also drop a like if you respect jaime's  devotion to banking and of course  consider joining our discord community  to suggest future video ideas and  consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hurry and i'll see you guys on  the next one

